WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.787
Welcome back to Building
the Daily Checks Workbook.

2
00:00:02.787 --> 00:00:06.638
This is Chapter 4: deploying
to test and production.

3
00:00:06.638 --> 00:00:10.003
The same commands twice, with
a different settings file,

4
00:00:10.003 --> 00:00:12.125
and a record of each step in between.

5
00:00:12.125 --> 00:00:13.740
It's a runbook, not a lab:

6
00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:18.225
it runs Chapters 2 and 3's scripts
and the kit's deploy scripts.

7
00:00:18.225 --> 00:00:22.092
The output you'll see comes
from the kit's fixture mode,

8
00:00:22.092 --> 00:00:24.113
on November sixteenth, 2026.

9
00:00:24.113 --> 00:00:27.153
Nothing here has been run
against a live subscription,

10
00:00:27.153 --> 00:00:30.160
and the chapter has been
reviewed and approved.

11
00:00:33.910 --> 00:00:35.735
Why treat a dashboard like this?

12
00:00:35.735 --> 00:00:38.020
Because it's still a change to the estate,

13
00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:42.660
and an unreviewed change is exactly what
the rest of the workbook guards against.

14
00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:45.682
So every deployment is
previewed with what-if,

15
00:00:45.682 --> 00:00:48.385
which predicts the change and makes none.

16
00:00:48.385 --> 00:00:52.823
A second person approves the plan's
hash, and the kit deploys nothing else.

17
00:00:52.823 --> 00:00:55.922
And every step leaves a
hashed, chained record.

18
00:00:55.922 --> 00:00:59.094
One rule holds throughout:
the workbook itself,

19
00:00:59.094 --> 00:01:03.030
and any screenshot of it, is
never on the evidence list.

20
00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:08.330
Here's what a stage holds.

21
00:01:08.330 --> 00:01:10.292
Preflight runs six checks.

22
00:01:10.292 --> 00:01:13.830
The validation records the
template's checks and its hash.

23
00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:18.067
The plan writes the parameters, the
what-if result and the plan file.

24
00:01:18.067 --> 00:01:21.405
The deployment writes its result
beside the approved plan.

25
00:01:21.405 --> 00:01:24.130
Reachability records each table's age.

26
00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:26.855
The rollback plan is
written before it's needed.

27
00:01:26.855 --> 00:01:31.250
And the evidence step records
each folder, hashed and chained.

28
00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:35.925
Who does what?

29
00:01:35.925 --> 00:01:39.924
The deployer needs Workbook Contributor,
which can save shared workbooks,

30
00:01:39.924 --> 00:01:42.310
and deployment rights
on the resource group,

31
00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:45.987
because a Resource Manager deployment
and its what-if both need them.

32
00:01:45.987 --> 00:01:49.139
Both are granted through
privileged identity management,

33
00:01:49.139 --> 00:01:50.812
for the deployment window only.

34
00:01:50.812 --> 00:01:54.163
A second person reads the
plan and approves its hash.

35
00:01:54.163 --> 00:01:57.624
The reachability check needs
only Log Analytics Reader.

36
00:01:57.624 --> 00:02:01.265
And readers need Workbook Reader
and their own access to the data,

37
00:02:01.265 --> 00:02:05.200
because a workbook shows only what
its reader can already query.

38
00:02:08.950 --> 00:02:10.725
Step 4 is the heart of it.

39
00:02:10.725 --> 00:02:13.496
The plan writes this
environment's parameters,

40
00:02:13.496 --> 00:02:17.404
the workspace and the parameter
values the template leaves empty,

41
00:02:17.404 --> 00:02:21.525
then runs what-if and prints the create
command and the hash to approve.

42
00:02:21.525 --> 00:02:27.488
In fixture mode, on November sixteenth, it
predicts one create: a single workbook.

43
00:02:27.488 --> 00:02:29.800
A first deployment shows one create,

44
00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:32.962
and a redeploy shows a
modify of the same workbook.

45
00:02:32.962 --> 00:02:36.537
Anything else, and any
delete, stops the stage.

46
00:02:36.537 --> 00:02:39.946
Then a second person reads the
plan and the what-if result,

47
00:02:39.946 --> 00:02:42.780
and records the hash in the change record.

48
00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:49.568
Step 6 deploys, and
only the approved plan.

49
00:02:49.568 --> 00:02:54.054
Without the Apply switch, the command prints
what it would run and changes nothing.

50
00:02:54.054 --> 00:02:55.692
With it, it deploys.

51
00:02:55.692 --> 00:03:00.301
And it refuses a plan folder that changed,
a hash that isn't the approved one,

52
00:03:00.301 --> 00:03:02.568
a plan with no what-if or with a delete,

53
00:03:02.568 --> 00:03:06.043
and a plan that wasn't made
against the live environment.

54
00:03:06.043 --> 00:03:08.368
Step 7 is the smoke test, in the portal:

55
00:03:08.368 --> 00:03:13.175
open the Daily checks workbook under
the test workspace, check that the ten

56
00:03:13.175 --> 00:03:18.650
tabs open with the banner, and that the
telemetry tab lists every expected table.

57
00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:26.488
Step 8 runs the reachability
check against the test workspace.

58
00:03:26.488 --> 00:03:29.363
Step 9 plans the rollback
before you need it.

59
00:03:29.363 --> 00:03:32.463
Step 10 records each folder as evidence.

60
00:03:32.463 --> 00:03:37.330
Then the go or no-go: the stage is a go
when every file matches its hash list,

61
00:03:37.330 --> 00:03:41.206
the chain is unbroken, and the
preflight, validation, plan,

62
00:03:41.206 --> 00:03:45.413
deployment and reachability each
have a passing live record.

63
00:03:45.413 --> 00:03:47.249
Fixture records never count.

64
00:03:47.249 --> 00:03:52.170
A no-go is a finding with an owner, and
the stage is repeated after the fix.

65
00:03:55.920 --> 00:03:59.507
Production runs the same commands
with the production settings file.

66
00:03:59.507 --> 00:04:02.292
That file names the test
stage's evidence folder,

67
00:04:02.292 --> 00:04:06.120
and the preflight refuses production
until that evidence passes.

68
00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:09.170
There are two ways back,
both planned in advance.

69
00:04:09.170 --> 00:04:13.215
Redeploy the previous approved
plan when the content is wrong;

70
00:04:13.215 --> 00:04:15.082
its hash is already approved.

71
00:04:15.082 --> 00:04:17.794
Or delete the workbook
when it shouldn't exist.

72
00:04:17.794 --> 00:04:23.433
Deleting it removes a view only: no
log data, alert rule or task changes.

73
00:04:23.433 --> 00:04:27.412
And on day two, a portal edit comes
back as an export, is validated,

74
00:04:27.412 --> 00:04:31.910
and replaces the template through
review before it's redeployed.

75
00:04:35.660 --> 00:04:37.547
Here's what each record supports.

76
00:04:37.547 --> 00:04:41.473
Preflight supports least privilege
and configuration settings.

77
00:04:41.473 --> 00:04:44.934
The validation supports the
baseline and change control.

78
00:04:44.934 --> 00:04:49.585
The plan and its approval support
change control and impact analysis.

79
00:04:49.585 --> 00:04:52.897
The deployment supports change
control and the baseline.

80
00:04:52.897 --> 00:04:56.911
Reachability supports continuous
monitoring and system monitoring.

81
00:04:56.911 --> 00:05:01.048
The rollback plan supports change
control and the contingency plan.

82
00:05:01.048 --> 00:05:03.512
And the evidence step supports continuous

83
00:05:03.512 --> 00:05:06.523
monitoring and the protection
of audit information.

84
00:05:06.523 --> 00:05:10.335
The records support these controls;
the assessor decides them.

85
00:05:10.335 --> 00:05:12.764
Thanks for following the four chapters;

86
00:05:12.764 --> 00:05:16.940
the workbook itself remains a view,
and the records are the evidence.
